  "id": "Compound::DB00343 - Disease::DOID:10763",
  "verbalization": "Diltiazem, a calcium channel antagonist, is implicated in the treatment of hypertension through its pharmacological action on calcium channels in vascular smooth muscle. This mechanism is supported by its inclusion in the class of calcium channel blockers, which are known to reduce vascular resistance and lower blood pressure. The evidence from multiple paths indicates that Diltiazem, through its classification as a calcium channel antagonist, is linked to the treatment of hypertension, with Nifedipine and Verapamil also being included in this class and showing similar therapeutic effects. The lowest common ancestors of Diltiazem and its derivatives—such as Calcium Channel Blocker and Aromatic Ether—suggest a shared biological pathway, reinforcing the mechanistic link between the drug and its therapeutic effect. The consistent scores across the paths indicate a strong, well-documented relationship, supported by the pharmacological classification and clinical outcomes. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of calcium channel modulation in the pathophysiology of hypertension, with Diltiazem serving as a representative member of this class.",
